## 2. Design ambiguities resolved at PLAN (binding on BUILDER)

The design is mechanically realizable except at three points, each of which would otherwise
be resolved by BUILDER improvisation. Resolved here so the frozen artifact is not decided by
an implementer's guess.

**C-1 — T8's literal reading is contradictory; use the `envelope_base` reading.**
D§7 T8 says every ingest-profile valid fixture "also validates against the stored profile."
Taken literally this can never pass: `#stored` **requires** `confidence` +
`confidence_derivation` (D§2.3 `stored_envelope`), and `#ingest` **forbids** both (D§2.3
`ingest_envelope`, DD-7 branches). The intent is stated in the same line — *"`#ingest`
accepts ⊆ what `envelope_base` accepts (restriction, not divergence)"*. **Binding reading:**
T8 asserts that every `ingest`-profile valid fixture validates against
`{"$ref": "#/$defs/envelope_base"}`, i.e. the ingest profile is a restriction of the shared
base and not a divergent sibling. T8 must **not** validate ingest fixtures against
`stored_envelope`.

**C-2 — normative precedence inside the design.** Where D§5's prose and D§2.3's schema text
disagree, **D§2.3 and the D§6.2 pointer column govern.** Specifically: D§5 INV-1 describes
`#ingest` as setting `"confidence": false` / `"confidence_derivation": false`; that is
pre-DD-7 shorthand. The schema text uses `{"not": {"required": [...]}}` branches, and the
D§6.2 pointer for fixture 03 (`allOf/2/not`) depends on it. Transcribe **D§2.3**.

**C-3 — pointer column: re-derive, never weaken.** The D§6.2 `absolute_schema_path` values
were observed in-design (D§2.4) against representative documents, on the design session's
`jsonschema` build. Path prefixes emitted around a root-level `$ref` are a library detail,
not a schema property. **Binding rule:** if the observed pointers differ under the version
actually installed at WU-2, BUILDER **re-derives the column and records the diff in the
IMPLEMENT report**; BUILDER must **not** relax T3 to substring/prefix matching, to
`validator` name matching, or to "≥1 error" — that would destroy exactly the anti-false-
positive property T3 exists for (D§7 note 3, D§6.2 closing note). Any re-derivation is
reported, because the pointer column is also what pins the schema text against silent
re-encoding.

